1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,905
On the day, the book is down 567 dollars.

7
00:00:27,905 --> 00:00:33,545
State Street SPDR S&P Biotech ETF (XBI) leads, +5.9 percent.

8
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:36,084
Citigroup (C) lags at -3.5.

9
00:00:36,084 --> 00:00:42,101
The checklist carries 1 item; details are in the written report.

10
00:00:42,101 --> 00:00:45,627
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:53,106
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:53,106 --> 00:01:01,030
12 positions, led by Banner, ticker BANR, Citigroup, ticker C, Cathay General Bancorp, ticker CATY.

13
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:11,914
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:11,868 --> 00:01:17,066
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:17,066 --> 00:01:19,665
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:19,665 --> 00:01:31,081
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:31,081 --> 00:01:35,350
Inside the book today: 4 positions up, 7 down.

18
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:37,856
Best, XBI at +5.90 percent.

19
00:01:37,856 --> 00:01:39,527
Worst, C at -3.46.

20
00:01:39,527 --> 00:01:44,910
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:52,614
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.28% per trade over 189 graded positions.

22
00:01:52,614 --> 00:02:00,225
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:02:00,225 --> 00:02:05,794
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:05,794 --> 00:02:19,159
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 246 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:19,113 --> 00:02:21,348
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:21,348 --> 00:02:30,570
Day 33 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:36,810
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 102,473.

28
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:42,306
Then the index fund, in gray, 0.1 thousand back at 102,367.

29
00:02:42,306 --> 00:02:49,478
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 2.8 thousand back at 99,650.

30
00:02:49,478 --> 00:02:52,086
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:52,086 --> 00:02:56,371
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:56,371 --> 00:03:01,214
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:03:01,214 --> 00:03:03,077
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:03,077 --> 00:03:11,985
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

35
00:03:11,947 --> 00:03:19,282
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

36
00:03:19,282 --> 00:03:21,523
Nothing here is financial advice.

37
00:03:21,523 --> 00:03:24,512
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
